<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
  <title>F.R.I.E.N.D. Proposal</title>

  <style>
    :root {
      --blue:#0057b8;
      --red:#d71920;
      --yellow:#f4c400;
      --white:#ffffff;
      --dark:#1a1a1a;
      --light:#f5f7fb;
      --gray:#e6e9ef;
    }

    * {
      box-sizing: border-box;
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
    }

    body {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      background: var(--white);
      color: var(--dark);
      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    header {
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--blue), #003d82);
      color: white;
      text-align: center;
      padding: 40px 20px;
      border-bottom: 8px solid var(--yellow);
    }

    header h1 {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 42px;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
    }

    header h2 {
      margin: 10px 0 0;
      font-weight: normal;
    }

    .ks-logo {
      background: white;
      color: var(--blue);
      display: inline-block;
      padding: 10px 18px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      font-weight: bold;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
      border: 3px solid var(--yellow);
    }

    nav {
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
      z-index: 10;
      background: var(--red);
      padding: 10px;
      text-align: center;
      box-shadow: 0 3px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.2);
    }

    nav a {
      display: inline-block;
      color: white;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: bold;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      margin: 4px;
      border-radius: 6px;
    }

    nav a:hover {
      background: var(--yellow);
      color: var(--dark);
    }

    section {
      padding: 35px 20px;
      max-width: 1200px;
      margin: auto;
    }

    h2 {
      color: var(--blue);
      border-left: 8px solid var(--yellow);
      padding-left: 12px;
    }

    .hero {
      background: var(--light);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gray);
    }

    .dashboard {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
      gap: 18px;
      margin-top: 25px;
    }

    .card {
      background: white;
      padding: 22px;
      border-radius: 14px;
      border-top: 6px solid var(--blue);
      box-shadow: 0 4px 14px rgba(0,0,0,.12);
    }

    .card h3 {
      margin-top: 0;
      color: var(--blue);
    }

    .metric {
      font-size: 34px;
      font-weight: bold;
      color: var(--red);
    }

    details {
      background: white;
      border: 1px solid var(--gray);
      border-left: 8px solid var(--blue);
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 18px;
      margin: 16px 0;
      box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.08);
    }

    summary {
      cursor: pointer;
      font-size: 20px;
      font-weight: bold;
      color: var(--blue);
    }

    ul {
      padding-left: 22px;
    }

    .chart {
      margin: 20px 0;
      background: var(--light);
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 20px;
      border: 1px solid var(--gray);
    }

    .bar {
      height: 28px;
      background: var(--blue);
      margin: 10px 0;
      border-radius: 6px;
      color: white;
      padding-left: 10px;
      line-height: 28px;
      font-weight: bold;
    }

    .bar.red { background: var(--red); }
    .bar.yellow { background: var(--yellow); color: var(--dark); }

    footer {
      background: var(--dark);
      color: white;
      text-align: center;
      padding: 30px 20px;
      margin-top: 40px;
    }

    .contact {
      background: var(--blue);
      color: white;
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 25px;
      margin-top: 30px;
    }

    .button {
      display: inline-block;
      background: var(--red);
      color: white;
      padding: 12px 18px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: bold;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }

    .button:hover {
      background: var(--yellow);
      color: var(--dark);
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>

<header>
  <div class="ks-logo">KING SOOPERS</div>
  <h1>F.R.I.E.N.D. PROPOSAL</h1>
  <h2>Full Retail Integration Engaged Network Dynamics</h2>
  <p>The D2 Developers are proud to introduce F.R.I.E.N.D.!</p>
</header>

<nav>
  <a href="#dashboard">Dashboard</a>
  <a href="#prepilot">Pre-Pilot</a>
  <a href="#cost">Pre-Pilot Cost</a>
  <a href="#pilot">Pilot Program</a>
  <a href="#regional">Regional Rollout</a>
  <a href="#division">Division Rollout</a>
  <a href="#roi">ROI</a>
  <a href="#inaction">Inaction</a>
  <a href="#redteam">Red Team</a>
  <a href="#rollout">10-Point Rollout</a>
  <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
</nav>

<section class="hero" id="dashboard">
  <h2>Executive Dashboard</h2>
  <p>
    F.R.I.E.N.D. is designed as an integrated retail intelligence system that supports store leaders,
    department leaders, and associates through clearer communication, task visibility, operational
    prioritization, training support, and performance insight.
  </p>

  <div class="dashboard">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Operational Visibility</h3>
      <div class="metric">155</div>
      <p>Division stores connected through one scalable operational framework.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Pilot Scope</h3>
      <div class="metric">3</div>
      <p>Low, medium, and high-volume stores used to test real-world performance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Rollout Path</h3>
      <div class="metric">20-30</div>
      <p>Regional rollout stores after pilot validation.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Primary Goal</h3>
      <div class="metric">ROI</div>
      <p>Reduce inefficiency, increase execution, and strengthen leadership decisions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="prepilot">
  <h2>1. Pre-Pilot</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>Purpose</summary>
    <p>
      The pre-pilot phase prepares the division for a controlled test of F.R.I.E.N.D. before any large
      financial or operational commitment is made.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Required Steps</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Define pilot objectives and measurable success standards.</li>
      <li>Select three test stores: low, medium, and high volume.</li>
      <li>Identify participating departments.</li>
      <li>Map current task, communication, production, and training workflows.</li>
      <li>Determine technology needs such as TC-52 devices, dashboards, and access points.</li>
      <li>Create leadership reporting templates.</li>
      <li>Train store leaders and department leaders on pilot expectations.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Success Metrics</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Task completion improvement.</li>
      <li>Reduced missed communication.</li>
      <li>Improved associate onboarding clarity.</li>
      <li>Improved department execution.</li>
      <li>Reduction in manual follow-up from leadership.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="cost">
  <h2>2. Pre-Pilot Cost</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>Cost Categories</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Software design and prototype build.</li>
      <li>Device readiness and TC-52 compatibility testing.</li>
      <li>Training material development.</li>
      <li>Leadership labor for planning and testing.</li>
      <li>Data collection and reporting setup.</li>
      <li>Security, permissions, and compliance review.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <div class="chart">
    <h3>Estimated Pre-Pilot Cost Distribution</h3>
    <div class="bar" style="width:80%">Software / Prototype</div>
    <div class="bar red" style="width:60%">Training / Labor</div>
    <div class="bar yellow" style="width:45%">Device Readiness</div>
    <div class="bar" style="width:35%">Reporting / Analytics</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="pilot">
  <h2>3. Pilot Program</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>3-6 Month Pilot Design</summary>
    <p>
      The pilot should run for 3 to 6 months across three stores representing different operating
      conditions. This allows leadership to understand how F.R.I.E.N.D. performs across multiple
      complexity levels.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Store Types</summary>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Low-volume store:</strong> tests simplicity, adoption speed, and labor-light execution.</li>
      <li><strong>Medium-volume store:</strong> tests everyday operational balance.</li>
      <li><strong>High-volume store:</strong> tests pressure, speed, communication load, and scalability.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Data Collection</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Task completion rates.</li>
      <li>Time to complete priority tasks.</li>
      <li>Associate training completion.</li>
      <li>Department leader adoption.</li>
      <li>Store leader visibility and decision speed.</li>
      <li>Feedback from associates and leaders.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="regional">
  <h2>4. Regional Rollout</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>20-30 Store Expansion</summary>
    <p>
      After pilot validation, F.R.I.E.N.D. can expand to a controlled regional rollout of 20 to 30 stores.
      This phase tests scale, training consistency, technical stability, and leadership adaptation.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Regional Goals</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Confirm pilot results across more store types.</li>
      <li>Build regional support structure.</li>
      <li>Refine training and troubleshooting guides.</li>
      <li>Identify department-specific differences.</li>
      <li>Prepare for full division deployment.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="division">
  <h2>5. Division Rollout</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>Full Division Implementation</summary>
    <p>
      The division rollout expands F.R.I.E.N.D. across all stores after pilot and regional validation.
      This stage requires structured leadership ownership, system support, training rhythm, and
      ongoing performance measurement.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Division Requirements</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Clear rollout calendar.</li>
      <li>Store leader onboarding.</li>
      <li>Department leader playbooks.</li>
      <li>Associate training modules.</li>
      <li>Support escalation process.</li>
      <li>Monthly performance reviews.</li>
      <li>Continuous improvement loop.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="roi">
  <h2>6. ROI Analysis</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>ROI Drivers</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Reduced wasted labor time.</li>
      <li>Improved task execution.</li>
      <li>Reduced missed production opportunities.</li>
      <li>Improved onboarding speed.</li>
      <li>Better leadership visibility.</li>
      <li>Reduced operational inconsistency between stores.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <div class="chart">
    <h3>ROI Opportunity Areas</h3>
    <div class="bar" style="width:90%">Labor Efficiency</div>
    <div class="bar red" style="width:75%">Execution Consistency</div>
    <div class="bar yellow" style="width:65%">Training Improvement</div>
    <div class="bar" style="width:55%">Shrink / Waste Reduction</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="inaction">
  <h2>7. Cost of Inaction</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>Potential Losses from Inaction</summary>
    <p>
      The cost of inaction is not only measured by direct dollars. It also appears through missed
      execution, unclear communication, inconsistent training, leadership blind spots, associate
      frustration, and lost operational time.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Hidden Costs</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Repeated communication breakdowns.</li>
      <li>Unclear task ownership.</li>
      <li>Inconsistent department standards.</li>
      <li>Slow onboarding for new associates.</li>
      <li>Lost leadership time spent chasing updates.</li>
      <li>Reduced ability to identify problems early.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="redteam">
  <h2>8. Red Team Analysis</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>Potential Risks</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Low associate adoption if the system feels complicated.</li>
      <li>Leadership resistance if reporting creates more work instead of less.</li>
      <li>Technology friction with devices or login access.</li>
      <li>Data accuracy concerns if tasks are not updated correctly.</li>
      <li>Rollout fatigue if training is too fast.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Risk Controls</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep the interface simple.</li>
      <li>Use store-level feedback before scaling.</li>
      <li>Train department leaders first.</li>
      <li>Measure only what matters.</li>
      <li>Build a clear support and correction process.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="rollout">
  <h2>9. 10-Point Rollout Plan</h2>

  <details open>
    <summary>Rollout Steps</summary>
    <ol>
      <li>Finalize proposal and executive approval.</li>
      <li>Select pilot stores.</li>
      <li>Map workflows and current pain points.</li>
      <li>Build prototype and dashboard structure.</li>
      <li>Prepare TC-52/mobile interface testing.</li>
      <li>Train store and department leaders.</li>
      <li>Launch 3-store pilot.</li>
      <li>Collect and analyze results.</li>
      <li>Expand to 20-30 store regional rollout.</li>
      <li>Scale to full division after validation.</li>
    </ol>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="contact">
  <h2>Contact</h2>

  <div class="contact">
    <h3>Zachariah Rhett</h3>
    <p>Email: Zachariah.rhett@stores.kingsoopers.com</p>
    <p>Phone: (303) 729-4161</p>
    <p>
      Proposal prepared for King Soopers division consideration.
    </p>
    <a class="button" href="#dashboard">Back to Dashboard</a>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  <p>F.R.I.E.N.D. Proposal | Full Retail Integration Engaged Network Dynamics</p>
  <p>The D2 Developers are proud to introduce F.R.I.E.N.D.!</p>
</footer>

</body>
</html>